# Accessibility

- Use semantic HTML: `button` for actions (not `div` + `onClick`); `nav`, `main`, `header`, `footer` where appropriate.
- Preserve heading order: one logical primary heading per view when it matters; do not skip levels (`h1` → `h2` → `h3`).
- Interactive controls must be keyboard reachable; keep focus visible. If removing default outline, provide an equivalent focus style.
- Forms: associate `<label>` with controls, or use `aria-label` / `aria-labelledby` when a visible label is not possible.
- Images: `alt=""` for decorative images; short descriptive `alt` for meaningful content.
- Do not rely on color alone; add text, icons, or patterns for state and errors.
- For heavy animation or motion, respect `prefers-reduced-motion` and match patterns already used in the codebase.

```tsx
// ❌ BAD
<div onClick={onClick} className="cursor-pointer">Click</div>

// ✅ GOOD
<button type="button" onClick={onClick} className="...">
  Click
</button>
```
